<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eifel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eifel is a low mountain range in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, extending into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Its volcanic landscape includes crater lakes known as maars. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and early modern period it was divided among many small territories, including the abbey of Prüm and the counties of Manderscheid and Blankenheim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eifel.jpg" alt="Eifel" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User Walter Koch on de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eifelblick.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
